00:22:57.080 --> 00:22:58.980
or five, as early as I can remember.

00:22:58.980 --> 00:22:59.813
- [Rhett] Right.

00:22:59.813 --> 00:23:02.320
- But that was a meaningful connection.

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:05.970
I had a relationship with Mister Rogers.

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:07.890
- I watched the documentary.

00:23:07.890 --> 00:23:10.540
What year did that stop?

00:23:10.540 --> 00:23:11.426
Did you--

00:23:11.426 --> 00:23:14.585
- It kept going for a long
time but I did outgrow it.

00:23:14.585 --> 00:23:16.387
- You outgrew it.
- I did outgrow it.

00:23:16.387 --> 00:23:21.387
- I was a fan like most kids
but it didn't pop into my head

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:24.172
when I thought about most influential.

00:23:24.172 --> 00:23:26.070
- I felt like I couldn't miss it.

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:29.230
I felt like he would know
if I didn't watch the show.

00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:33.151
I felt as if I had a
relationship with the guy.

00:23:33.151 --> 00:23:37.170
- I think there was
something about feeling like

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:39.530
I was being taught.
- Mm-hmm.

00:23:39.530 --> 00:23:41.746
- The parts I liked and remembered about

00:23:41.746 --> 00:23:44.190
Mister Rogers' Neighborhood
was when he would go into,

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:46.340
what was the name of the land?

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:48.030
Whatever land.
- I can't remember.

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:50.560
- And the puppets.

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:51.660
Even though--
- Pretend land?

00:23:51.660 --> 00:23:54.942
- Even though he was in those
moments still teaching me,

00:23:54.942 --> 00:23:58.000
I don't know, maybe it's
my issue with authority.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:23:59.870
When it was just a man sitting
there just saying things

00:23:59.870 --> 00:24:03.140
and trying to teach me
lessons, I'd be like boring.

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:05.360
Old man, sweater.

00:24:05.360 --> 00:24:07.820
And then I would be, oh,
puppets, now those are cool.

00:24:07.820 --> 00:24:09.854
That's just how I
interacted with the show.

00:24:09.854 --> 00:24:12.905
- I liked him a lot
better than the puppets.

00:24:12.905 --> 00:24:14.800
- Hmm, you were scared of
the puppets a little bit,

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:15.633
weren't you?

00:24:16.760 --> 00:24:17.650
A lot of kids were.

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:19.200
- I just didn't like puppets, man.

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:21.790
- 'Cause he did all the voices
and some of them were freaky.

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:23.950
- I could feel there was a hand in there.

00:24:23.950 --> 00:24:25.612
Just felt weird, that's wrong.

00:24:25.612 --> 00:24:26.790
That's wrong.

00:24:26.790 --> 00:24:28.700
Maybe there was a father
figure thing happening,

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:29.533
I don't know.

00:24:29.533 --> 00:24:33.603
- Oh Mister Rogers was
your stand-in father.

00:24:33.603 --> 00:24:34.990
- (chuckles) Yeah, let's say that.

00:24:34.990 --> 00:24:36.094
- Okay.

00:24:36.094 --> 00:24:38.554
- I got a kick out of
his son being interviewed

00:24:38.554 --> 00:24:40.150
for the documentary.

00:24:40.150 --> 00:24:42.210
Great documentary, one
of the best of last year.

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:43.920
I recommend you watch it
if you have any connection

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:44.800
to Mister Rogers.

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:46.383
What's your number nine?

00:24:46.383 --> 00:24:48.792
- The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air.

00:24:48.792 --> 00:24:50.154
- [Link] Uh-huh.

00:24:50.154 --> 00:24:54.230
- Of course you know that
many memes have been created

00:24:54.230 --> 00:24:55.710
about this, if you didn't happen to see it

00:24:55.710 --> 00:24:58.837
when it happened between 1990 and 1996.

00:24:58.837 --> 00:25:03.837
But for me, this was,
this is like the first,

00:25:04.612 --> 00:25:08.530
obviously I watched Growing
Pains and Family Ties

00:25:08.530 --> 00:25:10.653
and all those sitcoms coming up, but,

00:25:11.489 --> 00:25:12.970
this was the first one.

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:16.022
I remember watching the first episode.

00:25:16.022 --> 00:25:19.590
And just thinking, this is different, man.

00:25:19.590 --> 00:25:21.654
- Well it was for, it was the first one,

00:25:21.654 --> 00:25:25.700
I think to complete that
thought, that was made for us.

00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:30.390
Like somebody made a show
amongst all these other shows,

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:32.380
like Family Ties was for the family.

00:25:32.380 --> 00:25:34.720
Perfect Strangers is, you know--

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:36.566
- Perfect Strangers almost made my list.

00:25:36.566 --> 00:25:39.730
- I would almost, I would watch Frasier.

00:25:39.730 --> 00:25:40.772
These shows that like--

00:25:40.772 --> 00:25:41.605
- I was a fan of Frasier as well.

00:25:41.605 --> 00:25:43.410
- Again, there's only so
many shows, so many channels

00:25:43.410 --> 00:25:46.240
and nothing's on-demand
so you kinda had to watch

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:48.150
the stuff that was for
adults and just get in

00:25:48.150 --> 00:25:49.000
where you fit in.

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:52.130
I do remember that, I
don't know how we knew

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:53.020
that it was coming on.

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:54.530
I don't know how the marketing got to us,

00:25:54.530 --> 00:25:55.920
but I remember watching that first,

00:25:55.920 --> 00:25:57.563
expecting the first episode,

00:25:58.570 --> 00:26:01.950
having been such an
obsessive fan of their music.

00:26:01.950 --> 00:26:04.220
- Yeah, already fans of the music.

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:08.017
- And it's whoa, this is the
first show that's made for us.

00:26:08.017 --> 00:26:09.934
I'm explaining to my mom.

00:26:09.934 --> 00:26:13.240
This is the guy from Parents
Just Don't Understand.

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:14.530
- Right.
- Nightmare On My Street.

00:26:14.530 --> 00:26:17.650
- Because there was the whole Cosby Show,

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:20.590
then A Different World
and you kinda felt like

00:26:20.590 --> 00:26:22.610
A Different World was a little bit more,

00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:25.190
because it was college, it
was reaching a little bit more

00:26:25.190 --> 00:26:28.461
into our age group and I
watched it and liked it

00:26:28.461 --> 00:26:32.270
but this was all of a sudden,
it was the age that we were

00:26:32.270 --> 00:26:37.270
that in 1990, I was like 13.
- Mm-hmm.

00:26:38.375 --> 00:26:42.093
- And so when it started,
I was just about to begin

00:26:42.093 --> 00:26:44.750
this whole high school experience

00:26:44.750 --> 00:26:46.230
and so it was the perfect time.

00:26:46.230 --> 00:26:49.935
And I just thought everything
about it was perfect.

00:26:49.935 --> 00:26:52.743
It was incredibly, and
let me tell you right now.

00:26:52.743 --> 00:26:55.160
- Yeah.
- It does hold up.

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:57.800
Because my kids--
- It really does, yeah.

00:26:57.800 --> 00:27:01.410
- My kids got into watching
The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:04.330
a couple years ago when it was--

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:06.500
- Mine too.
- On whatever platform

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:07.960
it was on, you can still get it,

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:12.900
but they ended up watching
every single episode.

00:27:12.900 --> 00:27:17.080
In like a few months, like
every time I got home,

00:27:17.080 --> 00:27:19.350
they would be watching another
episode of The Fresh Prince

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:21.280
and then telling me about
what they were getting into

00:27:21.280 --> 00:27:23.297
and I would sit down and watch some of it,

00:27:23.297 --> 00:27:26.130
obviously it's ridiculous in a lot of ways

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:29.090
but for me, it changed the way I thought

00:27:29.090 --> 00:27:33.800
about half hour sitcom,
like studio-based sitcoms.

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:35.340
- Yeah.
- And exactly what you said.

00:27:35.340 --> 00:27:36.690
It felt like it was for me.

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:41.452
- And Will Smith, before he
started his YouTube channel,

00:27:41.452 --> 00:27:43.540
I think it's on another YouTube channel,

00:27:43.540 --> 00:27:46.572
it might have been right
when he, no, I take it back.

00:27:46.572 --> 00:27:50.323
When he started his YouTube
channel, he told a story.

00:27:51.159 --> 00:27:54.310
If you're into The Fresh
Prince, you gotta look this up.

00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:57.360
The story of The Fresh Prince being made.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:27:59.010
I kinda don't wanna spoil it but,

00:28:00.628 --> 00:28:03.770
my mind was blown by the fact that as big

00:28:03.770 --> 00:28:07.840
as a celebrity he was, like I
don't know if he won a Grammy,

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:11.400
Grammy-nominated, like he was broke.

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:16.400
And he tells this story of he's
over at Quincy Jones's house

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:22.680
at a party and Quincy Jones has the script

00:28:24.063 --> 00:28:25.950
or is talking to a guy who has the script.

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:27.360
I can't remember the story exactly,

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:29.740
but basically he puts him
on the spot to audition

00:28:29.740 --> 00:28:31.962
at the party and forces
him to do it even though

00:28:31.962 --> 00:28:35.351
he said I'm not ready, I
can't do this right now.

00:28:35.351 --> 00:28:38.130
And it was like this make or break moment

00:28:38.130 --> 00:28:42.750
for Will Smith even though
from my perspective,

00:28:42.750 --> 00:28:45.800
he was like one of the
biggest stars in the world

00:28:45.800 --> 00:28:50.799
because he was this
amazingly hilarious rapper.

00:28:50.799 --> 00:28:51.840
- Yeah.

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:54.210
- He was like the Mister
Rogers of rapping for me,

00:28:54.210 --> 00:28:57.059
like I felt like I had
a relationship with him.

00:28:57.059 --> 00:28:59.557
And, but he was broke,
he'd blown all the money.

00:28:59.557 --> 00:29:01.060
- He spent all his money, yeah.

00:29:01.060 --> 00:29:03.450
- Spent all the money
and he was desperate.

00:29:03.450 --> 00:29:07.401
And out of that desperation
came them rewriting the script

00:29:07.401 --> 00:29:11.241
that was written for
somebody else for him,

00:29:11.241 --> 00:29:13.260
and it became The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air.

00:29:13.260 --> 00:29:15.325
He tells the story great.

00:29:15.325 --> 00:29:17.832
I'll have to tweet it out.

00:29:17.832 --> 00:29:21.710
That is my number five.
- Oh wow.

00:29:21.710 --> 00:29:23.363
- So it's higher on my list.

00:29:24.287 --> 00:29:27.070
Man, it was just such a special time.

00:29:27.070 --> 00:29:30.571
That was back, and then that
next album was gonna come out

00:29:30.571 --> 00:29:33.216
which ended up being a flop
but we were like waiting

00:29:33.216 --> 00:29:35.770
for it and we didn't know when,

00:29:35.770 --> 00:29:38.510
we didn't have information.
- There was no internet.

00:29:38.510 --> 00:29:39.880
- So I remember waiting--
- Nobody's talking about this

00:29:39.880 --> 00:29:41.460
on the news.
- I remember going to--

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:44.080
- Especially if you didn't have cable.

00:29:44.080 --> 00:29:45.753
I didn't have cable at my
house so there's no MTV.

00:29:45.753 --> 00:29:48.430
- Yeah every weekend I'd
go to get my mom to take me

00:29:48.430 --> 00:29:51.840
to Record Bar and I would
just hope it would be there.

00:29:51.840 --> 00:29:52.870
And then I finally asked him

00:29:52.870 --> 00:29:54.160
and it turns out the people there,

00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:56.323
they knew when albums were coming out.

00:29:56.323 --> 00:29:58.970
They said, "Oh, three more
weeks, it's coming out."

00:29:58.970 --> 00:30:00.480
I couldn't wait.
- But to answer your question

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:02.450
about how we found out
about television shows,

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:04.229
it was commercials.

00:30:04.229 --> 00:30:07.360
You'd be watching a show and
there would be a commercial

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:09.220
and you would learn,
oh, there's a new show

00:30:09.220 --> 00:30:11.870
that's got The Fresh Prince in it.

00:30:11.870 --> 00:30:13.620
And you would be like,
when is that coming on,

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:14.760
and they would tell you
and you would watch it.

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:17.050
That was the only way you
found out about things.

00:30:17.050 --> 00:30:18.010
- Yeah.

00:30:18.010 --> 00:30:19.740
- 'Cause it wasn't like
there was billboards and--

00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:21.390
- And you could set your VCR to tape it

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:23.310
but I never did that.

00:30:23.310 --> 00:30:25.300
You'd miss 'em or you'd
get 'em on the re-run.

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:27.340
- Yeah we never ever did that.

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:32.250
- My number nine is, I
think you've alluded to it

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:35.257
later in your list, The Dukes of Hazzard.

00:30:35.257 --> 00:30:36.310
- [Rhett] Yes.

00:30:36.310 --> 00:30:38.860
- So--
- Number four for me.

00:30:38.860 --> 00:30:41.718
- Whoa, number four for you?
- Yeah.

00:30:41.718 --> 00:30:46.718
- Dukes of Hazzard ran from 1979 to 1985

00:30:47.030 --> 00:30:51.130
and I don't know at what point
Bo and Luke were replaced

00:30:51.130 --> 00:30:52.320
by their cousins.

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:54.700
- I mean in my mind it's pretty late.

00:30:54.700 --> 00:30:56.090
- I did not watch that.

00:30:56.090 --> 00:30:57.964
- No I tried.
- That was a huge betrayal

00:30:57.964 --> 00:31:00.982
of what I was interested in.

00:31:00.982 --> 00:31:03.410
I thought they were the same guys.

00:31:03.410 --> 00:31:06.528
I thought they did what
they did years later

00:31:06.528 --> 00:31:10.700
with The Fresh Prince's aunt.

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:12.850
Like the actress changed.

00:31:12.850 --> 00:31:14.050
- Yeah.
- After like,

00:31:14.050 --> 00:31:16.851
next season comes back it's
like, she's a different woman.

00:31:16.851 --> 00:31:18.725
- Aunt Viv.
- Aunt Viv.

00:31:18.725 --> 00:31:22.738
But anyway, I mean it was just southern,

00:31:22.738 --> 00:31:26.820
you got this General Lee
car which you can't do that.

00:31:26.820 --> 00:31:28.930
- No no.
- Like I don't even,

00:31:28.930 --> 00:31:30.640
I'm not even gonna say
that's a good thing.

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:35.640
- But I will say that half of the fan mail

00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:40.458
that was sent to the show was
addressed to the General Lee.

00:31:40.458 --> 00:31:41.908
(both chuckle)

00:31:41.908 --> 00:31:43.590
Fun fact.
- Seriously?

00:31:43.590 --> 00:31:45.890
- Yes.
- I mean that,

00:31:45.890 --> 00:31:49.820
that racist car was a character
on the show, wasn't it?

00:31:49.820 --> 00:31:51.540
- I had the car--
- it wasn't like KITT

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:53.364
from Knight Rider which
did not make my list.

00:31:53.364 --> 00:31:54.197
- It didn't have a personality.

00:31:54.197 --> 00:31:56.740
It had a Confederate flag
on the top of the car.

00:31:56.740 --> 00:31:59.350
- Right.
- And I had that car

00:31:59.350 --> 00:32:02.000
just to give you, this is
just the way we grew up,

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:06.483
I had that car, that
model car in my house,

00:32:06.483 --> 00:32:11.483
on my shelf and I had a bunch
of Dukes of Hazzard toys,

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:13.690
and I had a Dukes of Hazzard cup

00:32:13.690 --> 00:32:16.120
that I think I still have in my house.

00:32:16.120 --> 00:32:18.110
- We didn't call it Dukes of Hazzard.

00:32:18.110 --> 00:32:20.630
Like you said the first
time, Dukes o' Hazzard.

00:32:20.630 --> 00:32:21.888
- Dukes o' Hazzard.
- I didn't even know

00:32:21.888 --> 00:32:24.875
that Hazzard was a county really.

00:32:24.875 --> 00:32:29.875
I didn't, to me, Dukes o'
Hazzard was just one word

00:32:31.810 --> 00:32:36.810
that meant every Thursday
night, I go to my Nanny's house

00:32:37.438 --> 00:32:40.750
and we're gonna watch The Dukes of Hazzard

00:32:40.750 --> 00:32:41.950
and then when that's over,

00:32:41.950 --> 00:32:44.624
I have to sit there
while they watch Dallas

00:32:44.624 --> 00:32:47.450
which came on right after.
- Not quite the same.

00:32:47.450 --> 00:32:51.060
- That was not, I was
not into that at all.

00:32:51.060 --> 00:32:52.270
There was kissing on that.

00:32:52.270 --> 00:32:55.630
- The reason that it's so
high in my list is because,

00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:57.263
like The A-Team, it's
one of the first shows

00:32:57.263 --> 00:33:00.070
that I could remember being
appointment television

00:33:00.070 --> 00:33:05.070
for my family but The Dukes
o' Hazzard was the thing

00:33:05.680 --> 00:33:09.040
that I thought about as early
as I can remember television.

00:33:09.040 --> 00:33:11.938
And Cole and I, my brother and I would,

00:33:11.938 --> 00:33:15.131
sometimes we would dress
up like Bo and Luke.

00:33:15.131 --> 00:33:17.390
'Cause he had darker hair and I had,

00:33:17.390 --> 00:33:19.872
my hair was pretty blonde at the time

00:33:19.872 --> 00:33:24.143
and we would dress up like them
sometimes to watch the show.

00:33:24.143 --> 00:33:26.747
And then--
- You were cosplaying.

00:33:26.747 --> 00:33:30.900
- And then, so the show was
on, you said through 79 to 85,

00:33:30.900 --> 00:33:33.640
so when I moved to
California, now, you may,

00:33:33.640 --> 00:33:37.030
if you watch the show, you kinda
see the car running around,

00:33:37.030 --> 00:33:38.890
and you're like that's
not Georgia. (chuckles)

00:33:38.890 --> 00:33:42.860
That doesn't look like Georgia,
that looks like California.

00:33:42.860 --> 00:33:44.860
And of course I didn't know
what California looked like.

00:33:44.860 --> 00:33:48.747
We moved to California
in 1984 and my dad says,

00:33:48.747 --> 00:33:50.987
"I'm gonna take you to where they film

00:33:50.987 --> 00:33:52.610
"The Dukes of Hazzard."

00:33:52.610 --> 00:33:56.969
And so he took us out to, I
don't remember where it was,

00:33:56.969 --> 00:33:59.630
but it was the place that they would shoot

00:33:59.630 --> 00:34:02.460
all the car scenes, the
General Lee flying over

00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:05.220
in the river, doing all
the stunts and stuff.

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:07.610
- Yeah it was like demolition derby.

00:34:07.610 --> 00:34:09.130
- And it was just that--
- There would be a scene

00:34:09.130 --> 00:34:10.640
in every episode that would be like--

00:34:10.640 --> 00:34:11.660
- Oh yeah.

00:34:11.660 --> 00:34:13.430
- The car doing some epic jump.

00:34:13.430 --> 00:34:15.210
- The car would do a jump
and then it would pause

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:16.823
and then Waylon Jennings would say,

00:34:16.823 --> 00:34:20.929
"Now, these boys didn't know
what was about to happen."

00:34:20.929 --> 00:34:21.762
And then it would go to commercial--

00:34:21.762 --> 00:34:23.810
- It's so great.
- And then it would come back

00:34:23.810 --> 00:34:26.511
and un-pause the car and
then the car would land.

00:34:26.511 --> 00:34:30.353
I mean, everything about that
show at the time in my life

00:34:30.353 --> 00:34:35.220
and the way that I was
taking in entertainment,

00:34:35.220 --> 00:34:37.630
it just, it checked all the boxes.

00:34:37.630 --> 00:34:41.200
- It wasn't, I mean except
for the filming location,

00:34:41.200 --> 00:34:45.092
it was so distinctly southern.

00:34:45.092 --> 00:34:48.327
I felt like it was a documentary.

00:34:48.327 --> 00:34:51.060
When I turned off the television
and got back in the car,

00:34:51.060 --> 00:34:53.330
I was driving around in Hazzard County.

00:34:53.330 --> 00:34:55.470
- Well I don't know how
well you remember it

00:34:55.470 --> 00:34:58.290
because you remember
there's like Boss Hogg.

00:34:58.290 --> 00:35:00.780
Did you know anybody who
wore a all-white suit

00:35:00.780 --> 00:35:02.986
with a top hat?
- Almost.

00:35:02.986 --> 00:35:03.980
I'm telling you.

00:35:03.980 --> 00:35:07.920
- In my world it wasn't
quite that extreme.

00:35:07.920 --> 00:35:11.420
- It was, you know with
my relatives in my world.

00:35:11.420 --> 00:35:13.600
- Okay, I get it.
- I felt like it was just

00:35:13.600 --> 00:35:18.150
a reflection of reality, except
for the way the cars behaved

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:21.590
when they hit obstacles,
they would somehow just be

00:35:22.430 --> 00:35:25.560
ejaculated into the air.
- Whoa, okay.

00:35:25.560 --> 00:35:27.620
That's a word.
- Or projected into the air

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:28.880
is what maybe I should have said.

00:35:28.880 --> 00:35:30.250
- Yeah, they probably didn't use that word

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:31.649
on The Dukes of Hazzard.

00:35:31.649 --> 00:35:34.890
- I had a play set where
you'd rev up the car

00:35:34.890 --> 00:35:36.140
and you'd let it go on the thing

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:40.180
and it would go through a
barn and it had a ramp inside

00:35:40.180 --> 00:35:42.120
of the barn and when it came
out the other side of the barn,

00:35:42.120 --> 00:35:44.900
it would turn on its side

00:35:45.980 --> 00:35:49.240
and then do a barrel roll and land,

00:35:49.240 --> 00:35:52.600
and I had that set at
my Nanny's house too.

00:35:52.600 --> 00:35:54.730
So yeah we'd watch that together.

00:35:54.730 --> 00:35:58.083
I mean it was just, it was magical, man.

00:35:59.284 --> 00:36:01.420
- Well we should move on,
we've got a lot to go.

00:36:01.420 --> 00:36:04.414
