00:58:49.375 --> 00:58:53.910
and I mean, yeah that's
my favorite season.

00:58:53.910 --> 00:58:56.470
I think there was, it
was actually in the midst

00:58:56.470 --> 00:58:59.630
of a big transition period
'cause I kinda looked into this

00:58:59.630 --> 00:59:02.053
a little bit, 'cause I remembered,

00:59:02.053 --> 00:59:04.517
I remembered Chris Rock.

00:59:04.517 --> 00:59:07.480
And then, his last season was,

00:59:07.480 --> 00:59:10.860
I remember Norm Macdonald
hosting Weekend Update

00:59:10.860 --> 00:59:15.400
and that was the first season
that Chris Rock was gone.

00:59:15.400 --> 00:59:17.603
Norm was there the year
before but you know,

00:59:18.560 --> 00:59:20.800
and then Chris Farley coming in.

00:59:20.800 --> 00:59:23.560
Will Ferrell came in but it was

00:59:25.724 --> 00:59:30.670
when, why am I losing the name,

00:59:30.670 --> 00:59:32.700
the Canadian dude from Wayne's World.

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:33.533
What's his name?

00:59:33.533 --> 00:59:35.670
- Mike Myers.
- Yeah, Mike Myers.

00:59:35.670 --> 00:59:38.440
He was leaving, but a lot of people say

00:59:38.440 --> 00:59:42.340
it wasn't a strong point,
but for us it was all we had.

00:59:42.340 --> 00:59:43.340
- Yeah.
- And if you could,

00:59:43.340 --> 00:59:44.970
you'd stay up as late as you could

00:59:44.970 --> 00:59:45.803
and then we'd have to get up

00:59:45.803 --> 00:59:46.980
and go to church the next morning.

00:59:46.980 --> 00:59:51.540
We would skip out on Sunday
school, staying in the hallway

00:59:51.540 --> 00:59:53.176
and just talk about the sketches.

00:59:53.176 --> 00:59:54.578
- Yeah.
- You remember that?

00:59:54.578 --> 00:59:56.210
- Oh yeah--
- We just obsessed over

00:59:56.210 --> 00:59:59.593
the impersonation--
- We talked about this in the,

01:00:00.640 --> 01:00:02.350
earlier, I don't remember how long

01:00:02.350 --> 01:00:04.370
the Deep Thoughts by Jack Handey ran--

01:00:04.370 --> 01:00:05.203
- That's right.

01:00:05.203 --> 01:00:06.886
- But that was a big thing for us.

01:00:06.886 --> 01:00:08.105
- Yeah.

01:00:08.105 --> 01:00:11.897
- We actually just, that
was inserted in something

01:00:11.897 --> 01:00:14.488
that we're working on right
now that we'll talk about soon.

01:00:14.488 --> 01:00:15.840
- That's right, we put
a reference in there,

01:00:15.840 --> 01:00:18.118
and it's also in the--
- In the Book of Mythicality.

01:00:18.118 --> 01:00:21.430
- Yeah you see in the inserts
to our church bulletin,

01:00:21.430 --> 01:00:24.080
we would write our versions of
Deep Thoughts by Jack Handey.

01:00:24.080 --> 01:00:25.220
- That weren't actually funny.

01:00:25.220 --> 01:00:27.970
- It was some of our first comedy writing.

01:00:27.970 --> 01:00:31.690
- And then we met, who did we
meet that had a connection?

01:00:31.690 --> 01:00:34.530
- John Fortenberry directed
season one of Buddy System--

01:00:34.530 --> 01:00:35.363
- Yes.

01:00:35.363 --> 01:00:39.300
- And he worked on Deep Thoughts.

01:00:39.300 --> 01:00:40.133
- He worked on Deep Thoughts.

01:00:40.133 --> 01:00:41.080
- I was gonna say co-created

01:00:41.080 --> 01:00:42.560
but I don't know if that's true.

01:00:42.560 --> 01:00:44.380
He worked with--
- I think he worked with Jack,

01:00:44.380 --> 01:00:46.040
maybe directed those spots.

01:00:46.040 --> 01:00:48.300
- That's right 'cause Jack
Handey was a real person,

01:00:48.300 --> 01:00:50.010
a writer on SNL.

01:00:50.010 --> 01:00:52.248
- And Jack Handey, I looked him up

01:00:52.248 --> 01:00:53.813
when I was thinking about this,

01:00:53.813 --> 01:00:55.820
continues to write.

01:00:55.820 --> 01:00:59.220
He had a number of Deep Thoughts books.

01:00:59.220 --> 01:01:03.210
The first one published in 91 or 92,

01:01:03.210 --> 01:01:06.760
which is why it's referenced.

01:01:06.760 --> 01:01:11.760
And he's like self-publishing,
he kept doing it.

01:01:12.177 --> 01:01:15.690
And he's still a humorist
that does these absurd things,

01:01:15.690 --> 01:01:18.750
but anyway--
- Also Adam Sandler's songs.

01:01:18.750 --> 01:01:21.672
I mean you start to think
about, it really plants a seed,

01:01:21.672 --> 01:01:25.940
I believe in our minds that
like, we can write funny songs.

01:01:25.940 --> 01:01:26.773
We can do that.

01:01:26.773 --> 01:01:31.354
- It was really our only
exposure to sketch comedy.

01:01:31.354 --> 01:01:32.370
- Absolutely.

01:01:32.370 --> 01:01:34.140
- We didn't watch stand-up.

01:01:34.140 --> 01:01:37.900
I would watch stand-up on
a Showtime at the Apollo.

01:01:37.900 --> 01:01:40.370
Or I would watch like a Sinbad special.

01:01:40.370 --> 01:01:43.239
And I just thought that
Sinbad's comedy specials

01:01:43.239 --> 01:01:47.750
were the most incredible
thing I'd ever seen.

01:01:47.750 --> 01:01:49.550
- But very unapproacable.

01:01:49.550 --> 01:01:52.210
- We weren't really
students of comedy in that.

01:01:52.210 --> 01:01:55.830
We didn't have anybody,
stand-up wasn't a thing.

01:01:55.830 --> 01:01:58.810
We weren't listening to like
old tapes of George Carlin

01:01:58.810 --> 01:02:00.700
or whatever, a lot of
people coming up in comedy

01:02:00.700 --> 01:02:03.165
were getting that but we just
came from a different place,

01:02:03.165 --> 01:02:07.750
so we had NBC on Saturday night and--

01:02:07.750 --> 01:02:10.096
- If you could stay up, yeah
there was like this reward

01:02:10.096 --> 01:02:14.166
of like, oh if you can sneak
and stay up late enough

01:02:14.166 --> 01:02:15.530
and stay awake--

01:02:15.530 --> 01:02:17.590
- If you can stay up after SNL,

01:02:17.590 --> 01:02:19.400
you can watch Party in Progress.

01:02:19.400 --> 01:02:21.890
- Oh goodness, that's right.
- Remember that?

01:02:21.890 --> 01:02:23.459
- Oh yeah, it was just--
- Is that still a thing?

01:02:23.459 --> 01:02:24.750
- No.

01:02:24.750 --> 01:02:27.280
- Party in Progress--
- It was a half hour show.

01:02:27.280 --> 01:02:32.280
- No, all it was was a
bunch of girls in bikinis--

01:02:32.647 --> 01:02:34.640
- At parties.
- At parties

01:02:34.640 --> 01:02:35.707
and they would just film all of that.

01:02:35.707 --> 01:02:37.909
- I think they just went to Daytona Beach

01:02:37.909 --> 01:02:40.731
for like two weeks.
- In my 16 year old mind,

01:02:40.731 --> 01:02:45.023
it was the best thing that
anyone could have caught on film.

01:02:45.023 --> 01:02:47.850
It was revolutionary and I was hoping

01:02:47.850 --> 01:02:49.030
that no one would walk in.

01:02:49.030 --> 01:02:51.960
- It was just people
filming bikini contests

01:02:53.108 --> 01:02:56.410
at Daytona Beach and just
turning it into a show--

01:02:56.410 --> 01:02:58.480
- A show for us.

01:02:58.480 --> 01:02:59.680
- For people in colder regions.

01:02:59.680 --> 01:03:01.187
- Thought it was made just for me.

01:03:01.187 --> 01:03:03.702
I thought it was only
broadcast to my television.

01:03:03.702 --> 01:03:06.369
(Link chuckles)

01:03:07.350 --> 01:03:08.497
- I think I've told the story before,

01:03:08.497 --> 01:03:10.710
but I gotta mention it again.

01:03:10.710 --> 01:03:15.150
The Nielsen ratings people
sent worksheets to our house.

01:03:15.150 --> 01:03:19.080
For some reason my mom
agreed to participate--

01:03:19.080 --> 01:03:21.450
- I forgot about this.
- In the Nielsen ratings thing

01:03:21.450 --> 01:03:25.037
where you would write down
what shows you watched.

01:03:25.037 --> 01:03:28.087
Every night, you'd keep a log
and then you would mail in--

01:03:28.087 --> 01:03:30.202
- And you were literally a Neal's son.

01:03:30.202 --> 01:03:33.240
Which is, made it very appropriate.

01:03:33.240 --> 01:03:35.280
- Conflict of interest, perhaps.

01:03:35.280 --> 01:03:37.550
So we'd hand write the stuff we'd watch.

01:03:37.550 --> 01:03:40.220
We'd watch Entertainment
Tonight every night.

01:03:40.220 --> 01:03:41.450
And then we'd watch, I don't know,

01:03:41.450 --> 01:03:42.840
all the other stuff we watched,

01:03:42.840 --> 01:03:46.110
but then I remember my
mom set it up to mail it

01:03:46.110 --> 01:03:51.110
and I snuck, opened it and
added Party in Progress.

01:03:51.245 --> 01:03:53.580
(Rhett laughs)
Because I wanted--

01:03:53.580 --> 01:03:54.880
- In bold.
- I had to do--

01:03:54.880 --> 01:03:56.900
- Don't drop that Party in Progress.

01:03:56.900 --> 01:04:01.191
- I had to do my part to keep
Party in Progress in progress.

01:04:01.191 --> 01:04:02.736
(Rhett laughs)

01:04:02.736 --> 01:04:04.273
But I didn't want my mom to know.

01:04:04.273 --> 01:04:07.180
Uh, yeah.

01:04:07.180 --> 01:04:08.630
- Party in Progress is my number one.

01:04:08.630 --> 01:04:10.320
I'll just go ahead--
(Link laughs)

01:04:10.320 --> 01:04:12.438
Just go ahead and cut to the chase.

01:04:12.438 --> 01:04:14.290
- Oh man.
- No it did not make the list.

01:04:14.290 --> 01:04:17.620
- I gotta google that just
to see if it still exists.

01:04:17.620 --> 01:04:20.283
It's like, just bikini contests.

01:04:21.900 --> 01:04:22.960
That is my number two.

01:04:22.960 --> 01:04:27.870
I feel like it permeated
our comedic sensibility

01:04:27.870 --> 01:04:30.870
to the point that it
earned the next top spot.

01:04:30.870 --> 01:04:32.122
I'm just saying.

01:04:32.122 --> 01:04:36.080
- Yeah and this was not
easy, this wasn't easy, but--

01:04:36.080 --> 01:04:37.268
- I accept your apology.

01:04:37.268 --> 01:04:40.880
What is your next?
- My number two is

01:04:40.880 --> 01:04:42.006
The Wonder Years.

01:04:42.006 --> 01:04:43.390
- Oh.

01:04:43.390 --> 01:04:45.300
- Not even on your list.
- No.

01:04:45.300 --> 01:04:47.500
Never watched it but I knew
it would be on your list.

01:04:47.500 --> 01:04:52.500
- Okay so this ran from 1988
to 1993, starred Fred Savage.

01:04:52.816 --> 01:04:57.190
And the narrator was
Daniel Stern who was--

01:04:57.190 --> 01:04:58.550
- Really?
- Yeah.

01:04:58.550 --> 01:05:00.140
Every single episode--
- Your doppelganger

01:05:00.140 --> 01:05:02.920
from Home Alone.
- Yes, does all the narration

01:05:02.920 --> 01:05:07.160
as the adult version
of Kevin, Kevin Arnold,

01:05:07.160 --> 01:05:11.683
looking back on his life
growing up in the 60s and 70s.

01:05:13.480 --> 01:05:15.740
And it

01:05:17.634 --> 01:05:19.770
captured me.

01:05:19.770 --> 01:05:22.260
So all those things I said
about Northern Exposure

01:05:22.260 --> 01:05:24.020
are twice as true.

01:05:24.020 --> 01:05:27.535
So this is, I mean,
I'm talking, and again,

01:05:27.535 --> 01:05:31.673
this was the first show
that I watched by myself.

01:05:31.673 --> 01:05:33.210
- Okay.

01:05:33.210 --> 01:05:36.943
- And 'cause my parents
didn't care about this show.

01:05:36.943 --> 01:05:39.190
My brother was into other things,

01:05:39.190 --> 01:05:42.427
and so it just hit me at this time.

01:05:42.427 --> 01:05:45.780
And I'm watching it, so Kevin
is playing like a 12 year old,

01:05:45.780 --> 01:05:47.810
right, and I'm starting
to watch this thing

01:05:47.810 --> 01:05:49.880
in 1988 when I'm 11.

01:05:49.880 --> 01:05:50.713
- Yeah.

01:05:50.713 --> 01:05:53.418
- So he's got this
relationship with Winnie.

01:05:53.418 --> 01:05:55.210
- I remember Winnie.

01:05:55.210 --> 01:05:57.430
- He's got, she's really great at math.

01:05:57.430 --> 01:05:58.750
Super smart,

01:05:58.750 --> 01:06:00.013
and then he's got--
- She had bangs.

01:06:00.013 --> 01:06:02.930
- He's got his best friend Paul.

01:06:02.930 --> 01:06:07.204
And he's got his older brother
who was a bully to him.

01:06:07.204 --> 01:06:12.204
So many things about his
life were exact parallels

01:06:12.360 --> 01:06:14.503
to what I was going through.

01:06:14.503 --> 01:06:16.960
I had an older brother
who would hold me down

01:06:16.960 --> 01:06:20.540
and dangle spit over my face
just like his older brother.

01:06:20.540 --> 01:06:21.950
Wayne was his name.

01:06:21.950 --> 01:06:24.353
And--
- But you had a friend,

01:06:24.353 --> 01:06:25.720
that was me, I assume.

01:06:25.720 --> 01:06:27.080
- You were just like Paul.

01:06:27.080 --> 01:06:28.286
You didn't have glasses at the time.

01:06:28.286 --> 01:06:30.200
- Paul was like a--
- Nerd.

01:06:30.200 --> 01:06:31.853
- Nerd, I wasn't a nerd.

01:06:33.030 --> 01:06:35.490
- Well you may have not thought you were.

01:06:35.490 --> 01:06:36.880
But--
(chuckles)

01:06:36.880 --> 01:06:37.713
- What about a Winnie?

01:06:37.713 --> 01:06:39.060
You didn't have, who was your Winnie?

01:06:39.060 --> 01:06:40.800
- Well you always have a Winnie.

01:06:40.800 --> 01:06:41.790
You always have the girl--
- In your mind.

01:06:41.790 --> 01:06:44.430
- You have the girl that you're, for me,

01:06:44.430 --> 01:06:46.460
I always had a girl that I was fixated on.

01:06:46.460 --> 01:06:49.953
It just rotated and was a
failure nine times out of 10.

01:06:49.953 --> 01:06:51.450
- There was no comedy in
that, it was just drama.

01:06:51.450 --> 01:06:53.170
It was like, the feels.
- Wrong.

01:06:53.170 --> 01:06:54.140
There was comedy.

01:06:54.140 --> 01:06:55.520
It was funny.

01:06:55.520 --> 01:06:57.500
It was funny in exactly the same way

01:06:57.500 --> 01:06:58.990
that Northern Exposure was.
- Oh really?

01:06:58.990 --> 01:07:03.000
- Maybe not as quirky
as Northern Exposure.

01:07:03.000 --> 01:07:05.440
But no, it was funny.

01:07:05.440 --> 01:07:06.840
I have a feeling it holds up.

01:07:06.840 --> 01:07:08.540
I don't know, I haven't ever
gone back and watched it.

01:07:08.540 --> 01:07:09.670
Would love to watch it with the kids

01:07:09.670 --> 01:07:10.530
because it was already--

01:07:10.530 --> 01:07:13.135
- But you'd pretty much
cry every episode, right?

01:07:13.135 --> 01:07:18.030
- Well so what I remember is,

01:07:18.030 --> 01:07:21.560
I remember being in the
extra room, and again,

01:07:21.560 --> 01:07:24.640
just like weeping.

01:07:24.640 --> 01:07:27.440
Like, I was so invested.

01:07:27.440 --> 01:07:29.990
And crying to the point that I really hope

01:07:29.990 --> 01:07:32.210
that my mom doesn't need
anything from me right now

01:07:32.210 --> 01:07:34.410
because I'm so invested
in The Wonder Years.

01:07:34.410 --> 01:07:37.350
- That was the same feeling you
felt about Party in Progress

01:07:37.350 --> 01:07:38.710
but for different reasons.

01:07:38.710 --> 01:07:42.111
- Exactly, and I don't know,

01:07:42.111 --> 01:07:45.590
it was so well done and I felt like I was,

01:07:45.590 --> 01:07:48.823
interestingly I felt like I
was getting this insight into,

01:07:50.543 --> 01:07:52.630
I wasn't exactly getting insight

01:07:52.630 --> 01:07:56.513
into my parent's perspective
because they were,

01:07:57.670 --> 01:08:00.570
the generational gap, so I
was the age of the people

01:08:00.570 --> 01:08:03.560
playing the kids but it was from a time

01:08:03.560 --> 01:08:06.360
that was a little bit after
my parents were that age.

01:08:06.360 --> 01:08:07.193
- [Link] Mm-hmm.

01:08:07.193 --> 01:08:08.026
- I don't know how that would have

01:08:08.026 --> 01:08:09.770
worked out mathematically,
that would have been perfect,

01:08:09.770 --> 01:08:13.463
but I don't know, I just
felt like you got this,

01:08:15.216 --> 01:08:17.470
every character was so well developed

01:08:17.470 --> 01:08:19.738
and so relatable.
- Do you remember a moment

01:08:19.738 --> 01:08:23.430
like what was the defining moment?

01:08:23.430 --> 01:08:24.768
Do you remember the finale?

01:08:24.768 --> 01:08:27.475
- No, my brain doesn't work like that.

01:08:27.475 --> 01:08:31.970
I have a horrible memory when
it comes to the specifics.

01:08:31.970 --> 01:08:36.192
I just remember the vibe
and the general feel.

01:08:36.192 --> 01:08:40.897
But the way that he thought about Winnie.

01:08:41.970 --> 01:08:43.930
The way that he thought about girls

01:08:43.930 --> 01:08:47.790
and his friendships, it
was just so relatable,

01:08:47.790 --> 01:08:48.750
'cause you know me.

01:08:48.750 --> 01:08:52.870
I, at that age, at age 11 or 12,

01:08:52.870 --> 01:08:56.853
I was already completely
obsessed with girls.

01:08:58.050 --> 01:08:59.900
I wasn't a late bloomer.

01:08:59.900 --> 01:09:04.900
And so I already was
fixated on different girls

01:09:05.040 --> 01:09:06.980
and imagining what a relationship,

01:09:06.980 --> 01:09:08.420
oh if she was just my girlfriend,

01:09:08.420 --> 01:09:10.120
then everything would be great.

01:09:10.120 --> 01:09:11.189
And if she was my girlfriend,

01:09:11.189 --> 01:09:15.040
it would be the only
thing that I cared about.

01:09:15.040 --> 01:09:17.863
And I felt like he kind
of embodied that in a way,

01:09:17.863 --> 01:09:20.000
just the way that he was
dealing with everything.

01:09:20.000 --> 01:09:21.083
It was the most relatable thing

01:09:21.083 --> 01:09:23.500
and it was just incredibly well-done.

01:09:23.500 --> 01:09:25.210
- It was very dramatic.

01:09:25.210 --> 01:09:28.840
I just didn't have an appetite
for that drama at that point.

01:09:28.840 --> 01:09:29.850
- But you liked Northern Exposure

01:09:29.850 --> 01:09:31.460
but you liked Northern Exposure later.

01:09:31.460 --> 01:09:32.480
'Cause this happened earlier.
- Yeah, right.

01:09:32.480 --> 01:09:35.290
- 'Cause you would have been 10 years old

01:09:35.290 --> 01:09:36.550
or so when it started.

01:09:36.550 --> 01:09:39.960
- Well it seems that we
are each to our number one.

01:09:39.960 --> 01:09:43.134
And I pretty much know at this point

01:09:43.134 --> 01:09:46.680
that we agree, 'cause we both left off,

01:09:46.680 --> 01:09:48.980
it's just painfully obvious at this point

01:09:48.980 --> 01:09:51.660
that we agree on our number
one and isn't that fabulous?

01:09:51.660 --> 01:09:52.840
- Yes it is.

01:09:52.840 --> 01:09:57.500
- So our number one is Green Acres.

01:09:57.500 --> 01:09:59.640
I actually watched Green Acres.

01:09:59.640 --> 01:10:01.150
- I didn't.

01:10:01.150 --> 01:10:02.630
- I watched reruns of that.

01:10:02.630 --> 01:10:06.910
- Seriously, our number one is--

01:10:06.910 --> 01:10:08.140
- [Both] Seinfeld.

01:10:08.140 --> 01:10:09.741
- Absolutely.

01:10:09.741 --> 01:10:14.741
So Seinfeld ran from 1989 to 1998.

01:10:16.880 --> 01:10:20.503
So once it really hit its stride
after a couple of seasons,

01:10:21.396 --> 01:10:24.050
it was in our high school sweet spot

01:10:24.050 --> 01:10:26.520
when it was in its sweet spot.

01:10:26.520 --> 01:10:29.177
And I remember, again,
appointment viewing.

01:10:29.177 --> 01:10:32.180
I'd hunker down in my bed,
I'd get my homework done

01:10:32.180 --> 01:10:33.830
or I'd say, well it's gotta wait.

01:10:33.830 --> 01:10:38.830
And if, I would tell Mom
I'm watching Seinfeld

01:10:39.257 --> 01:10:42.310
in my room, hold my calls.

01:10:42.310 --> 01:10:43.481
(chuckles)

01:10:43.481 --> 01:10:46.310
I remember Michael Jubie
called me to do some homework

01:10:46.310 --> 01:10:49.280
and it was like, I answered
the phone 10 minutes

01:10:49.280 --> 01:10:53.460
before it came on and talked
to him for nine minutes

01:10:53.460 --> 01:10:54.790
and then I was like, hey dude.

01:10:54.790 --> 01:10:57.560
I know this conversation's
not anywhere near over

01:10:57.560 --> 01:11:00.010
but I gotta go 'cause
Seinfeld's coming on.

01:11:00.010 --> 01:11:02.355
I was not going to miss it.

01:11:02.355 --> 01:11:03.260
- Yeah.

01:11:03.260 --> 01:11:08.260
- It was so, the comedic
voice was so specific

01:11:08.650 --> 01:11:12.616
and so unique to anything
I had been exposed to.

01:11:12.616 --> 01:11:17.224
I think we were primed for
that Larry David cynicism,

01:11:17.224 --> 01:11:20.883
because though we didn't watch Letterman,

01:11:22.460 --> 01:11:24.506
I mean your dad had that dry wit

01:11:24.506 --> 01:11:26.530
and we resonated with that sense of humor.

01:11:26.530 --> 01:11:29.500
- I watched a little
Letterman, at the same time.

01:11:29.500 --> 01:11:30.900
- I just think that we were,

01:11:31.880 --> 01:11:36.650
that type of observational
dry sense of humor

01:11:36.650 --> 01:11:39.940
was something that really
resonated with us and we hadn't--

01:11:39.940 --> 01:11:41.600
- Well it was the kind thing where

01:11:41.600 --> 01:11:44.812
when they were riffing on something,

01:11:44.812 --> 01:11:47.840
you weren't just laughing at it,

01:11:47.840 --> 01:11:49.160
you were reveling in it.

01:11:49.160 --> 01:11:50.880
You know what I'm saying, so like,

01:11:50.880 --> 01:11:55.597
you were so blown away by the
choices that were being made

01:11:55.597 --> 01:11:57.764
and the way that the jokes were coming,

01:11:57.764 --> 01:12:00.630
the way that the jokes would just happen

01:12:00.630 --> 01:12:02.640
and then the way that the
whole story would come together

01:12:02.640 --> 01:12:04.290
at the end and these ridiculous plot lines

01:12:04.290 --> 01:12:07.840
would come together in
this completely stupid way.

01:12:07.840 --> 01:12:09.096
- Yeah.

01:12:09.096 --> 01:12:12.149
They would weave three stories.

01:12:12.149 --> 01:12:14.290
- It was like--
- And it would all pay off

01:12:14.290 --> 01:12:15.123
in one moment.

01:12:15.123 --> 01:12:19.100
- It was like grabbing something
that has the exact same

01:12:19.100 --> 01:12:22.120
vibrational energy, like
you grab onto something

01:12:22.120 --> 01:12:24.270
that is vibrating at the frequency

01:12:24.270 --> 01:12:26.780
that your body is vibrating
at and it creates this,

01:12:26.780 --> 01:12:29.040
and I know that sounds crazy, and listen--

01:12:29.040 --> 01:12:30.570
- But it felt, you could feel it.

01:12:30.570 --> 01:12:34.150
- Yeah it is, it transcended.

01:12:34.150 --> 01:12:37.300
And now, let me just address something.

01:12:37.300 --> 01:12:40.420
Now, Ethan of h3h3 did an incredible job

01:12:40.420 --> 01:12:44.174
of addressing this when he reacted to

01:12:44.174 --> 01:12:47.130
the react video.
- Teens react.

01:12:47.130 --> 01:12:49.280
- Where the teens were
reacting to Seinfeld

01:12:49.280 --> 01:12:52.853
and basically, just watch that.

01:12:54.630 --> 01:12:57.040
Just put h3h3 Seinfeld.

01:12:57.040 --> 01:12:58.516
- Yeah 'cause their basic premise--

01:12:58.516 --> 01:13:01.470
- And basically, I don't
remember every single thing

01:13:01.470 --> 01:13:04.830
that Ethan said but his
perspective is my perspective.

01:13:04.830 --> 01:13:08.700
And that is the fact that,
part of it was the fact that

01:13:08.700 --> 01:13:11.290
something that was being done on the show,

01:13:11.290 --> 01:13:14.500
modern kids felt like, it
made 'em feel uncomfortable,

01:13:14.500 --> 01:13:16.630
but they didn't get the joke.

01:13:16.630 --> 01:13:20.013
- It seemed culturally
insensitive and I'm sure it was

01:13:20.013 --> 01:13:24.333
at certain points but lots
of times, that was the joke.

01:13:24.333 --> 01:13:25.410
- Right.

01:13:25.410 --> 01:13:27.440
- They were making, it was commentary.

01:13:27.440 --> 01:13:28.273
- But a lot of people are like,

01:13:28.273 --> 01:13:30.002
I don't understand why it's funny.

01:13:30.002 --> 01:13:32.190
These characters seem ridiculous.

01:13:32.190 --> 01:13:35.430
Everybody's super selfish and
it's like exactly! (chuckles)

01:13:35.430 --> 01:13:38.750
- And I didn't, I couldn't
have articulated that,

01:13:38.750 --> 01:13:42.770
that like, okay, I mean they talked about

01:13:42.770 --> 01:13:44.640
how it's a show about nothing,

01:13:44.640 --> 01:13:48.050
because they made a show within the show.

01:13:48.050 --> 01:13:50.710
They conceptualized the
show within the show.

01:13:50.710 --> 01:13:51.543
- Right.

01:13:51.543 --> 01:13:53.443
- (chuckles) And then tried to sell it.

01:13:55.460 --> 01:13:56.770
Which again, that's just great.

01:13:56.770 --> 01:13:59.040
It's just so creative
and self-referential,

01:13:59.040 --> 01:14:03.870
but I couldn't have said,
well, Jerry doesn't change.

01:14:03.870 --> 01:14:05.890
Like, we're gonna break the rule, like,

01:14:05.890 --> 01:14:09.300
that was a ground rule that
I couldn't have articulated.

01:14:09.300 --> 01:14:12.940
It's like, well, for successful writing,

01:14:12.940 --> 01:14:14.310
the characters have to have an arc.

01:14:14.310 --> 01:14:15.320
- Nobody changed.

01:14:15.320 --> 01:14:17.110
- Nobody changes.
- Nobody grows.

01:14:17.110 --> 01:14:19.190
- Nobody's, they're all bad.

01:14:19.190 --> 01:14:20.637
They're bad people.
- Yeah, right.

01:14:20.637 --> 01:14:21.637
And they don't grow.

01:14:22.550 --> 01:14:24.330
They're all completely self-focused.

01:14:24.330 --> 01:14:29.330
- There are characters,
I mean look at House.

01:14:29.340 --> 01:14:33.407
A very comedically
self-centered flawed character

01:14:33.407 --> 01:14:35.910
but I dropped off, maybe he changes.

01:14:35.910 --> 01:14:38.100
It was like, they stuck
to their guns to the end.

01:14:38.100 --> 01:14:39.340
- Well I think that--
- Until it got kind

01:14:39.340 --> 01:14:41.912
of schmaltzy with this Green Day montage,

01:14:41.912 --> 01:14:44.100
but other than that--

01:14:44.100 --> 01:14:45.820
- But I think that the reason, again,

01:14:45.820 --> 01:14:47.550
this is completely subjective, right.

01:14:47.550 --> 01:14:48.540
This is very subjective.

01:14:48.540 --> 01:14:50.460
We're not talking about what
the greatest television shows

01:14:50.460 --> 01:14:52.200
are of all time, this
is the most influential

01:14:52.200 --> 01:14:55.544
and I think that even whatever
you think is the greatest

01:14:55.544 --> 01:14:57.887
is so subjective.

01:14:57.887 --> 01:15:00.310
It's that thing that I'm talking about.

01:15:00.310 --> 01:15:04.710
Some intersection of our
background and our sensibilities

01:15:04.710 --> 01:15:08.581
that were some weird combination
of nature and nurture

01:15:08.581 --> 01:15:12.530
primed us to be completely
ready to accept this.

01:15:12.530 --> 01:15:13.990
I think part of it had to do,

01:15:13.990 --> 01:15:15.913
and I think that you articulated the way

01:15:15.913 --> 01:15:17.570
that we think about it now with the fact

01:15:17.570 --> 01:15:20.820
that all these characters are
selfish and they don't change,

01:15:20.820 --> 01:15:23.897
we wouldn't have been able
to say that at the time,

01:15:23.897 --> 01:15:26.130
but one of the reasons
I think that appealed

01:15:26.130 --> 01:15:28.210
to us is that we were in this,

01:15:28.210 --> 01:15:32.380
the culture that we were growing
up in, we were good boys.

01:15:32.380 --> 01:15:35.090
And we were being told how to be good boys

01:15:35.090 --> 01:15:36.317
and the things that you should believe

01:15:36.317 --> 01:15:39.530
and the things that you should,
the way you should behave.

01:15:39.530 --> 01:15:43.836
And I think that all of a sudden,
these people from New York

01:15:43.836 --> 01:15:48.042
who were all single, didn't have kids,

01:15:48.042 --> 01:15:51.470
didn't aspire to get married or have kids,

01:15:51.470 --> 01:15:53.850
and were not playing by any of the rules

01:15:53.850 --> 01:15:56.490
that all the adults in
our lives were playing by.

01:15:56.490 --> 01:15:57.360
- Mm-hmm.

01:15:57.360 --> 01:16:00.474
- It was this opposites
attract thing, and like I said,

01:16:00.474 --> 01:16:04.928
but we had this yearning
for some kind of release

01:16:04.928 --> 01:16:09.910
and instead of going out and
doing irresponsible things,

01:16:09.910 --> 01:16:12.110
we watched Seinfeld.
- Like egging houses or--

01:16:12.110 --> 01:16:16.275
- We did some irresponsible
things, but we watched Seinfeld.

01:16:16.275 --> 01:16:19.047
I think SNL served the same
purpose in a lot of ways.

01:16:19.047 --> 01:16:21.970
It was like, it's a way to
kind of revel in this thing

01:16:21.970 --> 01:16:25.110
where you kinda need a
break from the setting

01:16:25.110 --> 01:16:27.890
that you live in.

01:16:27.890 --> 01:16:29.750
And I'm not saying that kids growing up

01:16:29.750 --> 01:16:32.155
in New York didn't like
Seinfeld, but for us,

01:16:32.155 --> 01:16:34.610
everything just lined up.

01:16:34.610 --> 01:16:37.500
And it's the way that we--

01:16:37.500 --> 01:16:39.200
- Who was your favorite character?

01:16:41.290 --> 01:16:43.513
- Whoa, that's a really good question.

01:16:44.500 --> 01:16:46.530
I mean I think it might be...

01:16:49.060 --> 01:16:53.730
It might be either Jerry
or George's dad. (laughs)

01:16:53.730 --> 01:16:57.431
- George's dad, who is Ben
Stiller's dad in real life.

01:16:57.431 --> 01:16:59.079
Kramer's my favorite character.

01:16:59.079 --> 01:17:00.730
- Oh yeah, love Kramer, of course.

01:17:00.730 --> 01:17:03.105
- Every time he entered,
everyone would cheer.

01:17:03.105 --> 01:17:04.530
- Yeah, well he was great.

01:17:04.530 --> 01:17:07.617
- 'Cause he would enter,
it was like a throwback.

01:17:07.617 --> 01:17:10.373
He was like a throwback to
an earlier time of comedy.

01:17:10.373 --> 01:17:12.570
- And he was also the only character

01:17:12.570 --> 01:17:17.265
who actually wasn't 100% selfish.

01:17:17.265 --> 01:17:19.300
He actually was a good friend.

01:17:19.300 --> 01:17:21.970
He was selfish at the same
time, but he was a good friend.

01:17:21.970 --> 01:17:23.160
- Yeah.

01:17:23.160 --> 01:17:25.300
He was also an ass man.

01:17:25.300 --> 01:17:28.600
- Yeah, well, so the--
- That license plate.

01:17:28.600 --> 01:17:29.995
- Jon Voight's car, right?

01:17:29.995 --> 01:17:31.128
- Yeah.

01:17:31.128 --> 01:17:35.352
- So this is something guys our age,

01:17:35.352 --> 01:17:38.563
people our age who work in comedy,

01:17:40.503 --> 01:17:43.674
you have this just the way
that Larry David thought

01:17:43.674 --> 01:17:46.520
kind of constantly
running through your mind

01:17:46.520 --> 01:17:48.980
and you're trying to make
sure that you're not just

01:17:48.980 --> 01:17:51.700
writing a scene or a joke that is,

01:17:51.700 --> 01:17:54.520
well that's just Seinfeld,
or that's what is now

01:17:54.520 --> 01:17:58.093
Curb Your Enthusiasm,
which is sort of a remix.

01:17:59.260 --> 01:18:01.210
It's so influential that
you just have to make sure

01:18:01.210 --> 01:18:03.260
that you're not going there all the time.

01:18:03.260 --> 01:18:04.700
And another reason you
don't wanna go there

01:18:04.700 --> 01:18:07.410
all the time is because, you know,

01:18:07.410 --> 01:18:09.580
20 year old people don't care about it,

01:18:09.580 --> 01:18:10.730
don't think it's funny.

01:18:11.660 --> 01:18:12.890
It's like they don't
find it funny anymore.

01:18:12.890 --> 01:18:14.880
'Cause things change, you're not wrong--

01:18:14.880 --> 01:18:16.180
- I was trying to figure out--
- You're different.

01:18:16.180 --> 01:18:18.970
- If my kids would be into it, like hey.

01:18:18.970 --> 01:18:20.800
You like Parks and Rec,
you like The Office.

01:18:20.800 --> 01:18:22.980
I started researching does it hold up?

01:18:22.980 --> 01:18:24.700
- They would not, my theory is.

01:18:24.700 --> 01:18:27.560
- Of course, people who
love it say it does hold up.

01:18:27.560 --> 01:18:29.540
But I'm gonna test it on my kids.

01:18:29.540 --> 01:18:31.590
I was talking to 'em about it last night.

01:18:32.629 --> 01:18:35.170
You know, Britton's a big influence.

01:18:35.170 --> 01:18:36.750
I'm gonna use him.
- Maybe it will hold up

01:18:36.750 --> 01:18:37.970
because--
- He said he loved it.

01:18:37.970 --> 01:18:39.810
So I betcha I can get my kids to love it.

01:18:39.810 --> 01:18:41.440
Here's the one thing that doesn't hold up.

01:18:41.440 --> 01:18:43.420
There's a lot of phone humor.

01:18:43.420 --> 01:18:45.660
And it's all written before the advent

01:18:45.660 --> 01:18:47.720
of cell phones and Google.

01:18:47.720 --> 01:18:50.520
Like specifically, I'm not
even saying the internet,

01:18:50.520 --> 01:18:52.800
but a lot of phone humor, a lot of--

01:18:52.800 --> 01:18:55.890
- Give me an example.
- Answering machine humor.

01:18:55.890 --> 01:18:56.810
That's just what I read.

01:18:56.810 --> 01:18:57.970
I don't remember an example.

01:18:57.970 --> 01:19:00.770
And then I was reading
an article that said

01:19:00.770 --> 01:19:02.440
if you're gonna introduce it to somebody,

01:19:02.440 --> 01:19:05.630
you should watch it in reverse.

01:19:05.630 --> 01:19:08.250
Like, start with season
19 or whatever it was,

01:19:08.250 --> 01:19:11.720
watch that forwards but
then watch 18, 17, 16.

01:19:11.720 --> 01:19:13.084
I don't know how many seasons.

01:19:13.084 --> 01:19:15.215
- It's not that many seasons
'cause it was a scripted show,

01:19:15.215 --> 01:19:16.048
I think there's just as many seasons

01:19:16.048 --> 01:19:17.990
as there are years, right?

01:19:17.990 --> 01:19:19.600
Typically that's how that's done.

01:19:19.600 --> 01:19:22.140
- Anyway, that brings
us to our number one.

01:19:22.140 --> 01:19:25.084
I do wanna, I don't think
it's on a streaming service,

01:19:25.084 --> 01:19:26.137
interestingly enough.

01:19:26.137 --> 01:19:27.596
- You can't watch Seinfeld anymore?

01:19:27.596 --> 01:19:30.260
- I think you gotta own it.

01:19:30.260 --> 01:19:31.093
- Nine seasons.

01:19:31.093 --> 01:19:32.880
- You have to get the nine seasons on DVD.

01:19:32.880 --> 01:19:33.940
It's at least what I've been told.

01:19:33.940 --> 01:19:36.210
We'll have to look into
that but all right,

01:19:36.210 --> 01:19:39.220
so we've completed our
list and you hung with us.

01:19:39.220 --> 01:19:42.576
This is a longer Ear Biscuit
but thanks for hanging with us.

01:19:42.576 --> 01:19:45.560
- And let us know what you
think about this format.

01:19:45.560 --> 01:19:47.420
It's something that we might do again.

01:19:47.420 --> 01:19:50.120
Okay, you can watch all
of Seinfeld on Hulu.

01:19:50.120 --> 01:19:51.600
Jacob just let us know.

01:19:51.600 --> 01:19:53.060
- Thank you, Jacob.

01:19:53.060 --> 01:19:55.862
- Let us know if you want,
what you thought about

01:19:55.862 --> 01:20:00.130
sort of this ranking,
reminiscing kinda thing

01:20:00.130 --> 01:20:01.810
'cause obviously we
can do this with things

01:20:01.810 --> 01:20:03.683
other than television shows.

01:20:04.580 --> 01:20:06.310
I enjoyed talking about it.

01:20:06.310 --> 01:20:08.010
- Yeah.

01:20:08.010 --> 01:20:11.419
So use #EarBiscuits, let
us know what you thought,

01:20:11.419 --> 01:20:14.430
and we'll speak at ya again next week.

